{"timestamp": "2026-11-04T16:58:09.689+00:00", "run_id": "20261104-165617-c44dcd", "agent": null, "session_id": null, "event": "checks_completed", "task_id": null, "handoff_id": null, "status": "ok", "duration_ms": null, "metadata": {"round": 1, "passed": true, "blocking_issues": [], "notes": ["The researcher marked at least one task incomplete", "Researcher notes: T1: I used the 2-search and 2-page limit, and read two Ofgem pages. Gaps: (1) I found no official source on how non-commodity charges (network, policy and levy costs) are passed through or fixed, or on whether 'fixed' contracts allow pass-through. (2) Nothing official on price-variation clauses. (3) No Citizens Advice or Energy Ombudsman business guidance was opened. (4) No typical termination-fee amounts or standing-charge figures. Further sources would be needed: Citizens Advice business energy pages, Energy Ombudsman, or Ofgem microbusiness licence conditions (SLC 7 and 7A).\nT2: Searches surfaced an Ofgem page, 'Third Party Intermediaries: what your microbusiness needs to know'. I did not open it because of the page limit, and it would be a good source for microbusiness broker commission rules. I did not open the licence conditions (SLC 7A and others), so details of end-of-contract notice timing, such as how far in advance suppliers must send renewal notices, are not covered beyond the general statements in S1. Citizens Advice business guidance was also not opened.\nT3: Gaps after using the page-open limit: (1) No official source read on VAT for business electricity (standard vs reduced rate, de minimis and charity supplies). (2) No official source on energy-intensive industry reliefs (British Industry Supercharger, EII exemption from policy costs, CCA eligibility criteria). (3) No Ofgem page on who sets each network and policy charge. (4) The only detail on the April 2026 network cost increases comes from the Ofgem blog, which gives no figures. S1 to S4 were read for earlier tasks and are not relevant to this one. Suggested follow-ups: Ofgem 'costs included in your bills' page, GOV.UK VAT on fuel and power guidance, and GOV.UK British Industry Supercharger guidance.\nT4: Gaps: (1) The current status of the Energy Bills Discount Scheme and any successor, such as the British Industry Supercharger, was not covered. (2) No official source read on how to verify green tariff claims (REGO backing, Ofgem green tariff guidance). (3) No source on meter types (half-hourly versus non-half-hourly) or guidance on comparing quotes like-for-like beyond Ofgem's general points. (4) S7 is dated and out of step with current schemes. Suggested follow-ups: Ofgem's green energy tariff guidance, GOV.UK business energy support pages, Citizens Advice business energy pages, and the Energy Saving Trust or Carbon Trust."]}, "error": null}
